#block(above: 1.05em, below: 0.42em)[#text(size: 13pt, weight: "bold")[Latin vs. Cyrillic]]
This is one of the biggest differences between these and often the one that missionaries fear the most. Latin is what we use in English. Normal normal. Cyrillics is the one that my mom says \"looks like Russian.\" But guess what?!?! In all honesty, it just takes time and lots of patience. НЕ БРИНИТЕ!

You will be slower at reading in Cyrillics at first, but it will become so much easier if you keep working at it. I recommend a few things that worked for me.

#block(above: 0.42em, below: 0.5em)[
- Write sentences and other useful phrases you already know in Cyrillics. \(The 12 week work-packet in the AP drive is a great resource for this\)
- Read the Book of Mormon in Serbian OUT-LOUD daily.
- Put your flash cards \(Quizlet or physical\) in cyrillics.
]
These things \(which hopefully you are already doing haha\) are just for frequency. As you see it more and make that connection between the latin alphabet and cyrillc, it will become so much easier.

One last thing. All missionaries should learn Serbian Italics. \(This is that funny font that all the Scripture Chapter headings are written in.\) It is always awkward when you are called on to read and you can\'t read the chapter heading for Book of Mormon night. This isn\'t as important as the main alphabet, but definitely worth learning \(so you don\'t look like a bozo\)

#block(above: 1.05em, below: 0.42em)[#text(size: 13pt, weight: "bold")[Ekavian vs. Ijekavian]]
The second difference that I wanted to highlight is Ekavian vs Ijekavian. Unless you have lived in Serbian or certain places on the coast, you have spoken and been taught Ijekavian. The simplest way I can explain this is Croatian has a lot of words with \"ije\" and Serbian takes that out and replaces it with \"e.\"

The blue book has a lot of great resources on this and I will attach some of the most helpful references below for those who want to do some deeper reading.

Examples:

#block(above: 0.42em, below: 0.5em)[
- Ijekavian: Mlijeko, čovjek, podijeliti, lijepo
- Ekavian: Mleko, covek, podeliti, lepo
- dijete - dete
- tijelo - telo
- lijevo - levo
- pjesma - pesma
- zvijezda - zvezda
- riječ - reč
- mlijeko - mleko
- lijek - lek
- sijati - sejati
- bijela - bela
]
Feel free to use these examples as needed.

Unfortunately, there are a lot of words that make this change. Because of this, I can\'t just put a list on here of all the words. You will have to learn them as you speak with people and as you read through the blue book.

#block(above: 1.05em, below: 0.42em)[#text(size: 13pt, weight: "bold")[Vocabulary Differences]]
Serbian is kinda like that friend who\'s always picking up English words and using them in everyday convo. It\'s like they\'re just chill with the whole mix and match thing. Why\'s that? Well, it\'s partly \'cause Serbian peeps are just more open to grabbing words from English compared to their Croatian buddies.

More often than not, if you can think of a hard\/very specific word in english, its probably the same in Serbian. Most medical terms - yeah prolly the same. Anything job specific - yeah prolly the same. I list some examples, but this will help you increase your vocabulary a ton. AS A CAUTION: THIS ISN\'T ALWAYS TRUE. Sometimes they will have their own words for it. Croatian likes to be different and separate themselves from Serbian as much as possible. So more than likely, the Croatian word is completely different. Serbs will joke about this all the time. Here are some examples where the Serbian word might seem more straightforward or logical compared to its Croatian counterpart:

Telescope:

#block(above: 0.42em, below: 0.5em)[
- Serbian: \"teleskop\"
- Croatian: \"zvjezdarnica\" \(observatory\)
]
Helicopter:

#block(above: 0.42em, below: 0.5em)[
- Serbian: \"helikopter\"
- Croatian: \"zrakoplov\" \(air cutter\)
]
Elevator\/Lift:

#block(above: 0.42em, below: 0.5em)[
- Serbian: \"lift\"
- Croatian: \"dizalo\"
]
Train Station:

#block(above: 0.42em, below: 0.5em)[
- Serbian: \"železnička stanica\"
- Croatian: \"željeznički kolodvor\"
]
Camera:

#block(above: 0.42em, below: 0.5em)[
- Serbian: \"kamera\"
- Croatian: \"fotoaparat\" \(photographic apparatus\)
]
Additionally, as you learn new words, you will notice that certain words are borrowed from different languages. This can actually be tied back to history and who ruled the area at the time. These \"rulers\'\" and their kingdoms had an impact on the languages. Serbian was influenced by the Orthodox Christian Byzantine Empire, while Croatian was influenced by the Roman Catholic Church and Western Europe.

When different empires ruled the region, they introduced new words and phrases into the local languages. For example, Serbian has more Turkish loanwords because of centuries of Ottoman rule, while Croatian has more Latin and German loanwords due to its connections with Western Europe.

Understanding this history helps with growing your vocabulary and help embrace the culture a little more. Helps you get to know the people you are teaching and where they came from a little better yk?

#block(above: 1.05em, below: 0.42em)[#text(size: 13pt, weight: "bold")[Double Conjugation]]
This is pretty straight forward. If you remember from the MTC, they should have taught you about double conjugation. If you would normally use the infinitive in Croatian, you can replace the Infinitive with \"da + the conjugated verb\" to make it Serbian \(for most cases\). This makes clitic order not as difficult in Serbian, as the clitic order resets every time a \"da\" is added.

Let\'s look at an example: \"I want to eat now.\" In Serbian, we would say \"želim da jedem sada,\" where \"da jedem\" is the double conjugation form, indicating \"I\" as the subject and \"eating\" as the action. In Croatian, we would say \"želim jesti sada,\" using the infinitive form of the verb \"jesti\" without any added endings.

More examples:

Serbian:

#block(above: 0.42em, below: 0.5em)[
- Ja volim da čitam knjige
- Ti želiš da ideš sa mnom
- Ona treba da spava više
]
Croatian:

#block(above: 0.42em, below: 0.5em)[
- Ja volim čitati knjige
- Ti želiš ići sa mnom.
- Ona treba spavati više.
]
#block(above: 1.05em, below: 0.42em)[#text(size: 13pt, weight: "bold")[Accent and Pronunciation]]
Stress:

#block(above: 0.42em, below: 0.5em)[
- Both Serbian and Croatian are syllable-timed languages, meaning that each syllable is pronounced with equal duration. However, the placement of stress may differ between the two languages.
- In Croatian, stress tends to be more fixed and typically falls on the first syllable of a word.
- In Serbian, stress placement can vary depending on factors such as word structure, suffixes, and regional dialects. It is generally less predictable compared to Croatian.
]
Intonation:

#block(above: 0.42em, below: 0.5em)[
- Croatian intonation tends to be more tonal, with a rising or falling pitch pattern that can convey different meanings or emotions.
- Serbian intonation is generally more flat or neutral compared to Croatian, with less variation in pitch.
]
Accentuation, speed, and flow:

#block(above: 0.42em, below: 0.5em)[
- In Croatian, there is often a strong emphasis on each syllable, with clear articulation and enunciation of sounds. It sounds a lot more sophisticated and words are easier to understand\/pick apart.
- In Serbian, accentuation may be more subdued, with less emphasis on individual syllables and a smoother flow of speech. Aka, the words are going to be a lot more blurred together, words are going to be slurred, and it is overall harder to understand.
]
