#block(above: 1.05em, below: 0.42em)[#text(size: 11pt, weight: "bold")[Systematic Differences]]
#degmark A few patterns worth knowing on top of what he already flagged, so you can recognize them across words you haven\'t memorized yet.

#strong[Infinitive vs. da + present.] This is his Double Conjugation section above — Serbian tends to swap the Croatian infinitive for \"da\" plus a conjugated verb. Not repeating the examples here, just flagging that it\'s the same pattern showing up everywhere, not just in the textbook-style sentences.

#degmark #strong[Verb endings for loanwords \(-irati \/ -isati \/ -ovati\).] When international or loan verbs get adapted into Serbo-Croatian, Croatian usage leans toward \"-irati\" while Serbian usage often shows up as \"-isati\" or \"-ovati\" instead — e.g. \"organizirati\" \(Croatian-leaning\) alongside \"organizovati\" \(Serbian-leaning\). Both forms circulate to varying degrees, so treat this as a tendency, not a hard rule for every verb.

#degmark #strong[Future tense spelling.] Croatian convention tends to write the future tense of a \"-ti\" infinitive as one fused word with the \"-ću\" clitic — e.g. \"gledat ću\" gets written\/spoken as \"gledaću\" in Serbian convention, where the clitic just gets tacked onto the verb stem directly instead of staying a separate word. Same future tense, different spelling convention for how the clitic attaches.

#degmark #strong[\"Da li\" vs. \"je li.\"] Both are used across the region to form yes\/no questions, but they carry different regional and register associations rather than being a strict Croatian\/Serbian split — don\'t treat this as a hard rule, just something to notice as you hear both in the wild.

